    a: "<b>Anti-CCP vs RF in RA diagnosis:</b><br><b>Rheumatoid Factor (RF):</b><ul><li>Antibody (usually IgM) against the Fc portion of IgG</li><li>Sensitivity for RA: ~70–80%; Specificity: ~80%</li><li>Also positive in: Sjögren's syndrome (&gt;70%), SLE, HCV hepatitis (cryoglobulinemia), sarcoidosis, TB, endocarditis, elderly normal subjects</li></ul><b>Anti-CCP (anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide):</b><ul><li>Antibody against citrullinated proteins (PAD enzyme converts arginine → citrulline)</li><li>Sensitivity: ~67%; <b>Specificity: ~95%+</b> — highly specific for RA (best available)</li><li>Precedes clinical RA by years (predictive)</li><li>Associated with more severe, erosive disease and extra-articular manifestations</li><li>Useful in <b>seronegative RF</b> patients (15% of RA patients)</li></ul><b>2010 ACR/EULAR RA Classification Criteria — serology:</b><ul><li>Low positive RF or anti-CCP (1–3× ULN): 2 points</li><li>High positive RF or anti-CCP (&gt;3× ULN): 3 points</li><li>≥6 points total (including joint, duration, acute phase reactants) = RA</li></ul>"
